WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.855 --> 00:00:05.533
An ERP is the business system for orders and purchasing, stock and costs,

2
00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:06.788
such as SAP.

3
00:00:07.773 --> 00:00:10.211
An MES is the system on the floor.

4
00:00:10.956 --> 00:00:15.228
It runs orders per line and records what is made, used and scrapped.

5
00:00:16.307 --> 00:00:17.758
It runs in phases.

6
00:00:18.497 --> 00:00:21.574
SAP's method is called SAP Activate.

7
00:00:22.321 --> 00:00:26.931
It calls the core phases prepare, explore, realize, deploy and run.

8
00:00:27.969 --> 00:00:31.336
Every vendor uses other names; the order is the same.

9
00:00:32.659 --> 00:00:36.119
Go-live is the day the new system becomes the official one.

10
00:00:36.799 --> 00:00:39.585
From then on new work goes only into it.

11
00:00:40.643 --> 00:00:44.788
The old one, the legacy system, is no longer used for new work.

12
00:00:45.569 --> 00:00:49.111
Everything points at that day, so choose the date carefully.

13
00:00:50.145 --> 00:00:51.318
There are two ways.

14
00:00:52.089 --> 00:00:56.036
In a big bang, all sites and departments switch on the same day.

15
00:00:57.064 --> 00:01:00.815
The advantage is one switchover, with no temporary links.

16
00:01:01.519 --> 00:01:04.538
The drawback is that everything is at risk at once.

17
00:01:05.846 --> 00:01:09.364
A phased rollout goes per site, per line or per module.

18
00:01:10.357 --> 00:01:12.099
It often starts with a pilot.

19
00:01:12.888 --> 00:01:16.998
One site or line goes first, so you learn before the rest follows.

20
00:01:18.029 --> 00:01:21.976
In factories an MES often goes per line or per plant.

21
00:01:22.791 --> 00:01:26.042
An ERP usually switches a whole site at once.

22
00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:31.624
Phased costs extra, with temporary interfaces and a longer project.

23
00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:34.951
That is the price of a smaller risk per step.

24
00:01:36.263 --> 00:01:37.679
Timing matters as much.

25
00:01:38.408 --> 00:01:41.833
In nineteen ninety-nine the American chocolate maker Hershey

26
00:01:41.903 --> 00:01:46.024
went live with a new ERP just as the Halloween orders came in.

27
00:01:47.042 --> 00:01:48.853
According to CIO magazine,

28
00:01:49.062 --> 00:01:53.230
Hershey could not deliver about a hundred million dollars of orders that season.

29
00:01:54.215 --> 00:01:56.293
So never go live in your peak season.

30
00:01:56.998 --> 00:02:01.329
Ask which month is the quietest, and whether go-live is planned there.

31
00:02:02.745 --> 00:02:06.623
A dairy group brings a new MES into three plants.

32
00:02:07.347 --> 00:02:09.623
Plant A makes twenty percent of the volume.

33
00:02:10.318 --> 00:02:11.526
December is the peak.

34
00:02:12.555 --> 00:02:15.829
Option one is a big bang in one weekend in October.

35
00:02:16.556 --> 00:02:20.898
If it goes wrong, all production is at risk, two months before the peak.

36
00:02:21.916 --> 00:02:23.274
Option two is phased.

37
00:02:24.041 --> 00:02:28.104
A pilots in March, B follows in June and C in September.

38
00:02:28.957 --> 00:02:32.277
A temporary interface costs forty thousand euros,

39
00:02:32.533 --> 00:02:35.006
and the project runs six months longer.

40
00:02:36.055 --> 00:02:37.739
The group chooses option two.

41
00:02:38.481 --> 00:02:42.777
If the pilot goes wrong, twenty percent is at risk instead of a hundred.

42
00:02:43.767 --> 00:02:47.634
C, the biggest, then has three months to settle before December.

43
00:02:48.949 --> 00:02:53.129
Data migration is moving data from the old system into the new one.

44
00:02:54.139 --> 00:02:57.854
Master data is the fixed information many orders use,

45
00:02:57.993 --> 00:03:00.803
such as articles and bills of materials.

46
00:03:01.805 --> 00:03:06.484
Open items are what is in progress on go-live day, such as open orders,

47
00:03:06.682 --> 00:03:08.435
stock and unpaid invoices.

48
00:03:09.473 --> 00:03:13.733
History, such as orders from past years, is usually not migrated.

49
00:03:14.489 --> 00:03:17.995
It stays readable in the old system or in an archive.

50
00:03:19.305 --> 00:03:23.751
First comes data cleansing, removing duplicates, unused articles,

51
00:03:23.938 --> 00:03:25.912
wrong units and empty fields.

52
00:03:26.643 --> 00:03:29.673
Rubbish in the old system becomes rubbish in the new.

53
00:03:30.711 --> 00:03:33.416
That is work for the business, who own the data.

54
00:03:34.172 --> 00:03:35.577
IT shows what is wrong,

55
00:03:35.681 --> 00:03:39.245
but not which of two near-identical articles is the real one.

56
00:03:40.261 --> 00:03:42.722
The migration itself has three steps.

57
00:03:43.432 --> 00:03:47.693
Extract from the old system, transform to the new structure, and load.

58
00:03:48.752 --> 00:03:53.687
You rehearse that in a mock migration, a trial run into a test environment.

59
00:03:54.441 --> 00:03:59.108
Each trial is cleaner and faster, and the last is timed for the go-live plan.

60
00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:02.984
After every load comes reconciliation.

61
00:04:03.802 --> 00:04:07.993
Compare the numbers in old and new, and explain every difference.

62
00:04:08.796 --> 00:04:12.511
How many went out, how many came in, what was rejected and why?

63
00:04:13.483 --> 00:04:16.420
The business signs off on the result, not IT.

64
00:04:17.114 --> 00:04:21.282
The controller signs for the stock value, the planner for open orders.

65
00:04:22.285 --> 00:04:26.860
Often the migration starts late, and cleaning only at the third trial.

66
00:04:27.616 --> 00:04:32.109
Then there is no time left, and dirty data goes live to be fixed later.

67
00:04:33.148 --> 00:04:37.537
Ask at every project meeting how many records failed in the last trial,

68
00:04:37.665 --> 00:04:39.128
and who owns fixing them.

69
00:04:40.443 --> 00:04:45.575
A metal parts maker moves to a new ERP; this is its second mock migration.

70
00:04:46.293 --> 00:04:48.522
Unused articles go to the archive,

71
00:04:48.697 --> 00:04:51.936
so nine thousand nine hundred and fifty should move.

72
00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:58.108
Nine thousand eight hundred and seventy-one arrive, so seventy-nine are rejected.

73
00:04:58.838 --> 00:05:03.470
Fifty-two have no unit, twenty-seven a drawing number that already exists.

74
00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:08.475
The stock value differs by thirteen thousand five hundred euros.

75
00:05:09.183 --> 00:05:12.445
That is exactly the stock of the rejected articles.

76
00:05:13.452 --> 00:05:17.689
Engineering fixes the duplicates, purchasing fills in the units,

77
00:05:17.852 --> 00:05:20.023
each with a date before the third trial.

78
00:05:20.732 --> 00:05:26.003
The target is zero rejected and zero euros difference, signed off by the controller.
